  "nl_text": "This database schema, named rschema_0446, is used to store information related to an agricultural monitoring and management system. It contains nine tables, each representing a different entity or aspect of the system.\nThe first table, \"user,\" stores information about the users of the system, including their usernames, passwords, phone numbers, registration times, last login times, and roles. The primary key for this table is the user ID.\nThe second table, \"sensor,\" stores information about the sensors used to collect data in the system, including their IDs, data collection times, soil moisture, temperature, rainfall, light intensity, sensor location, and burial depth. The primary key for this table is the sensor ID, and it has a foreign key constraint referencing the \"meteorology\" table.\nThe third table, \"meteorology,\" stores information about the meteorological conditions in the area where the sensors are located, including timestamps, temperatures, humidity, rainfall, wind speed, and light intensity. The primary key for this table is the meteorological ID.\nThe fourth table, \"crop,\" stores information about the crops being monitored, including their IDs, names, types, and associated growth models. The primary key for this table is the crop ID, and it has foreign key constraints referencing the \"meteorology\" and \"growth_model\" tables.\nThe fifth table, \"growth_model,\" stores information about the growth models used to predict crop growth, including their IDs, names, parameters, and creation times. The primary key for this table is the model ID.\nThe sixth table, \"user_water_and_fertilizer_supply,\" stores information about the water and fertilizer supplies provided to crops, including the admin ID, crop type, supply time, location, water supply, fertilizer supply, and associated sensor ID. The primary key for this table is the associated sensor ID and admin ID, and it has foreign key constraints referencing the \"sensor\" and \"user\" tables.\nThe seventh table, \"user_operation,\" stores information about the operations performed on crops, including the admin ID, crop ID, operation time, type, location, and details. The primary key for this table is the admin ID and crop ID, and it has foreign key constraints referencing the \"crop\" and \"user\" tables.\nThe eighth table, \"system_performance_evaluation,\" stores information about the performance of the system, including the admin ID, crop ID, evaluation time, metric name, value, and location. The primary key for this table is the admin ID and crop ID, and it has foreign key constraints referencing the \"crop\" and \"user\" tables.\nThe ninth table, \"model_performance_evaluation,\" stores information about the performance of the growth models, including the model ID, crop ID, evaluation metric name, value, and location. The primary key for this table is the model ID and crop ID, and it has foreign key constraints referencing the \"crop\" and \"growth_model\" tables.\nOverall, this schema provides a comprehensive and detailed representation of the agricultural monitoring and management system, with tables for users, sensors, meteorology, crops, growth models, water and fertilizer supplies, user operations, system performance evaluation, and model performance evaluation.",
  "gold_question": "Requirements:\n1.User Information: Each registered user has a unique username, password, phone number, registration time, last login time, and role. Users can supply water and fertilizer to crops through sensors, and the system needs to record the supply time, location, amount of water, and amount of fertilizer. Additionally, any other actions taken by the user on the crop (such as planting, fertilizing, etc.) need to be recorded, including the time, location, type, and details of the action. Users can also evaluate the crop’s growth performance through the system.\n2.Sensor Deployment: Sensors precisely measure meteorological data and record information such as the data collection time, soil moisture, temperature, rainfall, light intensity, sensor location, and burial depth. Each sensor can infer corresponding meteorological data.\n3.Meteorology: Each meteorological record includes timestamp, temperature, humidity, rainfall, wind speed, light intensity, and location.\n4.Crops: Each crop has a unique crop ID, name, type, and a matching growth model.\n5.Growth Model: Each crop has a corresponding growth model, which includes the model name, parameters, and creation time. The crop is evaluated using this model, and the evaluation records the name of the evaluation metric, its value, and the location of the evaluation.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_codellama13b/0446.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0446\nCREATE TABLE `user` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `phone_number` varchar(20) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `registration_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `last_login_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `role` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `sensor` (\n  `sensor_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `data_collection_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `soil_moisture` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `temperature` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `rainfall` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `light_intensity` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `sensor_location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `burial_depth` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `meteorology_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`sensor_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `sensor_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`meteorology_id`) REFERENCES `meteorology` (`meteorology_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `meteorology` (\n  `meteorology_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `timestamp` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `temperature` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `humidity` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `rainfall` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `wind_speed` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `light_intensity` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`meteorology_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `crop` (\n  `crop_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `growth_model_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`crop_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `crop_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`growth_model_id`) REFERENCES `growth_model` (`model_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `crop_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`meteorology_id`) REFERENCES `meteorology` (`meteorology_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `growth_model` (\n  `model_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `parameters` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `creation_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`model_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `user_water_and_fertilizer_supply` (\n  `admin_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `crop_type` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `supply_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `water_supply` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `fertilizer_supply` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `sensor_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`admin_id`, `sensor_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `user_water_and_fertilizer_supply_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`sensor_id`) REFERENCES `sensor` (`sensor_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `user_water_and_fertilizer_supply_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`admin_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `user_operation` (\n  `admin_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `crop_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `operation_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `details` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`admin_id`, `crop_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `user_operation_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`crop_id`) REFERENCES `crop` (`crop_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `user_operation_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`admin_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `system_performance_evaluation` (\n  `admin_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `crop_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `evaluation_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `metric_name` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `value` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`admin_id`, `crop_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `system_performance_evaluation_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`crop_id`) REFERENCES `crop` (`crop_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `system_performance_evaluation_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`admin_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `model_performance_evaluation` (\n  `model_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `crop_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `evaluation_metric_name` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `value` float DEFAULT NULL,\n  `location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`model_id`, `crop_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `model_performance_evaluation_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`crop_id`) REFERENCES `crop` (`crop_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `model_performance_evaluation_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`model_id`) REFERENCES `growth_model` (`model_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
